## Part (a)

We construct $G(x,x_0)$ satisfying $L(G)=\delta(x-x_0)$, $G(0,x_0)=0$, $G(L,x_0)=0$.

**Piecewise form.** For $x \neq x_0$, $G$ solves the homogeneous equation $L(G)=0$. On $[0,x_0)$ the only boundary condition is $G(0,x_0)=0$, so $G$ must be a multiple of $y_1$ (the unique homogeneous solution vanishing at $0$). On $(x_0,L]$ the only boundary condition is $G(L,x_0)=0$, so $G$ must be a multiple of $y_2$. Hence

$$G(x,x_0) = \begin{cases} A\, y_1(x), & x < x_0, \\ B\, y_2(x), & x > x_0. \end{cases}$$

**Matching conditions at $x=x_0$.**

1. *Continuity:* $A\,y_1(x_0) = B\,y_2(x_0)$.
2. *Jump in flux:* Integrate $L(G)=\delta(x-x_0)$ across $x_0$:
$$\int_{x_0-\epsilon}^{x_0+\epsilon}\!\frac{d}{dx}\!\big(p\,G'\big)\,dx + \int_{x_0-\epsilon}^{x_0+\epsilon}\!q\,G\,dx = 1.$$
As $\epsilon\to 0$, the second integral vanishes (since $G$ is bounded), giving
$$p(x_0)\big[G'(x_0^+)-G'(x_0^-)\big]=1,$$
i.e. $p(x_0)\big[B\,y_2'(x_0)-A\,y_1'(x_0)\big]=1$.

**Solving.** From continuity, $B = A\,y_1(x_0)/y_2(x_0)$. Substituting into the jump condition:

$$A\,p(x_0)\left[\frac{y_1(x_0)\,y_2'(x_0)}{y_2(x_0)} - y_1'(x_0)\right]=1,$$

$$A = \frac{y_2(x_0)}{p(x_0)\,W(x_0)}, \qquad B = \frac{y_1(x_0)}{p(x_0)\,W(x_0)},$$

where $W(x) := y_1(x)\,y_2'(x) - y_1'(x)\,y_2(x)$ is the Wronskian.

**Constancy of $pW$.** For $L(y)=(py')'+qy=0$, Abel's identity gives $(pW)'=0$, so $p(x)W(x)=p(x_0)W(x_0)=\text{const}$ for all $x$. This lets us evaluate the denominator at any convenient point.

**Result:**

$$\boxed{G(x,x_0) = \frac{1}{p(x_0)\,W(x_0)} \begin{cases} y_1(x)\,y_2(x_0), & x \le x_0, \\[4pt] y_1(x_0)\,y_2(x), & x \ge x_0, \end{cases}}$$

with $W(x_0)=y_1(x_0)y_2'(x_0)-y_1'(x_0)y_2(x_0)$, and $p(x_0)W(x_0)=p(x)W(x)$ (constant). The assumption $y_1\neq c\,y_2$ guarantees $W\not\equiv 0$, so the denominator is nonzero.

## Part (b)

If $y_1(x)=c\,y_2(x)$ for all $x$, then $W(x)\equiv 0$, so $p(x_0)W(x_0)=0$ and the formula diverges — the Green's function **does not exist**.

**Why:** Linear dependence of $y_1$ and $y_2$ means $y_1$ (which vanishes at $0$) is proportional to $y_2$ (which vanishes at $L$). Thus $y_1$ satisfies *both* boundary conditions simultaneously: $y_1(0)=0$ **and** $y_1(L)=c\,y_2(L)=0$. So the homogeneous boundary-value problem

$$L(y)=0,\quad y(0)=0,\quad y(L)=0$$

has a **nontrivial solution** $y_1$. This means $0$ is an eigenvalue of $L$ (with these BCs), the operator is **singular / non-invertible**, and the equation $L(G)=\delta$ has no solution — the inverse that the Green's function represents does not exist. Physically this is a **resonance** condition: the forcing at $x_0$ excites a zero mode of the system.

